The timeout covers the whole request, including reading the body, so a slow download can also trip it during `response.json()`. Keep the body read inside the same `try`.

`AbortSignal.timeout` is available in Node 18+ and all current browsers.

```js
function throttle(fn, wait) {
  let last = 0;
  let timer = null;
  return function (...args) {
    const run = () => {
      last = Date.now();
      fn.apply(this, args);
    };
    clearTimeout(timer);
    const remaining = wait - (Date.now() - last);
    if (remaining <= 0) run();
    else timer = setTimeout(run, remaining);
  };
}

window.addEventListener("scroll", throttle(onScroll, 200), { passive: true });
```

The first scroll event runs the handler immediately. Events inside the next 200 ms are held back, and the latest one runs when the window ends, so the handler always sees the final scroll position after scrolling stops.

